ADR-0016 section 4's ORIGINAL specified consumer of the fourth verdict, built
at last on the machinery INV-pojib landed this morning. Owner-authorized
2026-08-13 after the blocker-stack census showed this is the ONLY route to a
self-proof that can ever say anything.

WHAT `inconclusive` WAS CONFLATING. Two very different epistemic states reached
the same verdict:

  * "a whole language here has no catalogue -- I am blind"
  * "I examined every call and understood them all; three of them hand control
    to git / pip / rustup, and no static analysis can see inside a launched
    process"

The auditor distinction is exact: a DISCLAIMER ("could not form a view") versus
a QUALIFIED OPINION ("correct, except these named items"). hypergumbo issued
disclaimers where a qualified opinion was warranted -- and because it launches
programs BY DESIGN, plain `confirmed` was permanently unreachable for its own
self-proof, making that artifact one that could never say anything at all.

MEASURED on the shipped CLI, four fixtures that discriminate:

  launch is the sole blocker      inconclusive rc 2 -> CAVEATS rc 3, site named
  real I/O, no launch             confirmed rc 0    (unchanged)
  launch + a real leak            violated rc 1     (unchanged)
  launch + uncatalogued module    inconclusive rc 2 (unchanged)

The last is the one that matters: opacity QUALIFIES only when it is the SOLE
remaining blocker. Beside a genuinely uncatalogued module the silence is
ambiguous -- the reader cannot tell which gap produced it -- so the
qualification is withheld. `qualifying_only` is DERIVED inside
compute_boundary_coverage, beside the evidence, because a gate whose caller can
forget to arm it fails open (the INV-dabov lesson).

A FIFTH FIXTURE WAS A BAD CONTROL AND IS RECORDED AS SUCH: my first "no launch"
control was `return 1 + 1`, which produced NO CALL EDGES AT ALL and came back
inconclusive for an unrelated and correct reason (the empty-analysis blocker).
Rebuilt with real catalogued I/O (os.listdir/os.remove) it confirms at rc 0.
A control that fails for the wrong reason is not a control.

BOTH CLAIM KINDS, ONE DISCLOSURE. Boundary claims read the BoundaryCoverage
object directly; taint claims reach the gate through a flattened blind_reason
STRING, so the sites travel alongside it (`_taint_blind_reason` now returns
`(reason, opaque_sites)`). Both render through ONE builder, and a parity test
asserts byte-identical kind/entries/detail from the two paths -- two spellings
of one disclosure would drift the first time either was edited (L53).

THE TAINT PATH APPENDS, NEVER ASSIGNS. A verdict can already carry INV-pojib's
user_supplied_sanitizer kind, and the self-proof is exactly that case: it
declares a zone-barrier sanitizer AND shells out to git. Overwriting would be
the one-slot last-writer-wins class (INV-virat, closed hours ago) reappearing
inside the caveat list itself; a test pins that both kinds survive together.

THE DIRECTION IS TOWARDS CONFIRMING, which is why this needed authorization and
why it ships now rather than a week ago: its soundness rests entirely on the
launch list being COMPLETE, and that surface is what constructors-counted
(INV-motos), opacity-gated (INV-gahuz), producer-stamps-unerasable (INV-larol,
INV-virat) and row-order-masking-closed (INV-zumin) hardened.

WORDING IS DELIBERATE: "cannot see inside a launched program", never anything
implying full coverage. "I saw every call" is not "I saw every I/O" --
INV-vavup measured bash redirection writes emitting no edge at all.

THE SELF-PROOF DOES NOT MOVE YET, stated so this is not mistaken for closing
INV-dabuf: its front blocker is still bash (layer 1 of the four-layer stack),
re-confirmed 18/18 inconclusive on the cached survey after this change. This is
machinery landed ahead of its arming event, the same ordering WI-joluk's gate
was built under.

A DUPLICATE-RECORD DEFECT IN THE FIRST VERSION, found by the owner asking
whether the append avoids duplicate data. It did not. Measured before the fix:

    claims file holding BOTH a boundary claim and a taint claim
      B-1 caveat kinds = ['opaque_boundary', 'opaque_boundary']

`has_taint_claims` populates blind_reason for EVERY verdict, so verify_claim
attached the caveat to the boundary verdict and _require_coverage_to_confirm
appended a second copy. Not cosmetic: `caveats` is the machine surface a
consumer branches on and COUNTS, so a doubled entry says a claim rests on
twice as many unverifiable doors as it does.

THE LESSON IS THE MIRROR OF INV-virat, HOURS APART: switching assign -> append
is what stops a second writer erasing the first, and doing it unguarded lets
the SAME kind accumulate. One fix opened its own inverse. `_merge_caveat` is
now the single place a caveat joins a verdict; it folds by KIND, keeps the
UNION of entries (under-reporting unverifiable doors is the failure that
matters; over-reporting is merely noisy), and re-renders opacity's prose so a
widened list cannot leave the sentence saying "1 call site" over two. Two of
its branches are unreachable from today's callers -- both paths currently
produce identical site lists -- and are covered by direct unit tests rather
than pragmas.

A CONTROL THAT FAILED FOR THE WRONG REASON, recorded rather than replaced: my
first "no launch" control was `return 1 + 1`, which produced NO CALL EDGES AT
ALL and came back inconclusive via the empty-analysis blocker. Rebuilt with
real catalogued I/O (os.listdir / os.remove) it confirms at rc 0.

